course: dictionary
lesson: 13892
title: Cutting words: lacerate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lacerate is a formal word. To lacerate skin is to cut or tear it badly, so the cut is jagged, with rough, uneven edges. «The broken glass lacerated her hand.» The glass did not just nick her skin. It made a deep, ragged cut.
explain: Use the clues. Broken glass and a hand are things that can cut, and the sentence is about harm. Ask: what would fit here? Cut badly fits. Swap it in and check: «The broken glass cut her hand badly.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Lacerate can also describe feelings. «Her unkind words lacerated his feelings.» Nothing was cut, but the words hurt him deeply. This is the literary sense, so you will mostly meet it in stories and writing that uses formal language.
fact: Lacerate is a verb. Its other forms are lacerates, lacerated and lacerating.
fact: A laceration is the noun: a deep, jagged cut or tear in skin or flesh.
fact: «A lacerated hand» means a hand with a jagged cut or tear. A laceration needs cleaning and may need stitches to close it.
fact: Lacerate is used in first aid, in news reports about accidents, and in stories. In everyday talk, people often say «cut» or «tear» instead.
fact: When lacerate describes feelings, it means someone has been hurt deeply, even though no one was physically injured.
exercise: «The rough rocks lacerated his knees when he fell.» What does lacerated mean here? | Painted bright red | Gently stroked | *Cut or torn badly | Washed clean
exercise: A nurse says, «This laceration on her arm needs cleaning.» What is a laceration? | A purple bruise that does not hurt | *A deep, jagged cut or tear | A roll of bandage | A sneeze
exercise: «Her unkind words lacerated his feelings.» What does this suggest? | They cut his hair short | They made him laugh loudly | *They hurt him deeply | They helped him feel better
test: «The branches lacerated her arms as she ran through the wood.» Which word is closest in meaning to lacerated? | *Torn | Hugged | Dried | Lifted
test: Ruth reads, «The news report said the driver had lacerated his leg in the crash.» What happened to his leg? | It was tickled by a branch | It was painted | *It was cut or torn badly | It was measured for a new shoe
exam: «Harsh words can lacerate a friend's feelings, even when no one is physically hurt.» What does lacerate mean here? | Cut with a knife | Wrap in a bandage | *Hurt deeply | Tickle gently
